{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Learning Experience Design and Software Technology", "title": "Module 2 Design Memo", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting module 2 design memo to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology", "ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 8, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "08", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/02/07"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_calibration"]}, "content": "# Module 2 Design Memo\n\n![Module 2 Design Memo textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/02-08-module-2-design-memo.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA design memo is the document that turns a module's worth of decisions into a recommendation a stakeholder can act on. It is the capstone of Module 2 because it forces you to do something the individual sections did not: synthesize. The affordance map, the interaction choices, the feedback loop, the AI boundaries, and the comparative audit each produced a finding; the memo argues from those findings to a single, fundable decision. Designers who can build artifacts but cannot write the memo stall at the point where their work meets a budget holder.\n\nThe central skill is the difference between a report and an argument. A report recounts what you did (\"I studied affordances, built a storyboard, ran a pilot, compared tools\"). An argument states a claim, names the audience and decision, and ties each piece of evidence to the specific point it supports. The professional convention that enforces this is **bottom line up front (BLUF)**: the recommendation comes first, the reasoning follows, and every sentence after the recommendation exists to support or qualify it. A memo that buries its recommendation in the last clause has not yet become an argument.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA program lead has ten minutes and three memos to read before a funding meeting. The first memo opens: \"In Module 2, I studied platform affordances, interaction patterns, feedback loops, and AI boundaries. I built a storyboard, ran a small pilot, and compared a few authoring tools. Overall the project went well, and I recommend Tool A.\" The lead reaches the recommendation in the final clause, with no idea why Tool A, for whom, or against what. The work behind that memo may have been excellent; the memo wasted it.\n\nThe case shows that a memo is judged as a decision instrument, not as a record of effort. The activity log feels honest and complete, but it forces the reader to reconstruct the argument the writer should have made. A memo that makes a busy stakeholder do that work will lose to a shorter one that states the recommendation first and connects each piece of evidence to it. The memo's quality is measured by whether the reader can act, not by how much the writer did.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA decision memo has a fixed anatomy, and each part does a job. The **header** names the audience and the decision requested (\"To: Program Lead. Decision requested: approve Tool A for the Summer build\"), so the reader knows immediately what they are being asked to do. The **recommendation** states the bottom line in one sentence, including the chief alternative it beats (\"Fund Tool A, not Tool B, despite Tool B's larger template library\"), because naming the rejected option signals that a real comparison happened. The **evidence**, then, is structured as claim-to-support links: each piece (the audit score, the pilot result, the budget) is attached to the specific point it justifies, not dumped as a list. Finally, the **trade-off acknowledged** section names what the recommendation costs and why that cost is acceptable, which is what distinguishes a credible argument from a sales pitch.\n\nThe hardest move is the claim-to-evidence link, because Module 2 generated a pile of findings and the temptation is to recite them all. Discipline means using only the evidence that bears on the decision. The accessibility gate from the comparative audit and the pilot's low-vision result both bear directly on the Tool A versus Tool B choice; a finding about narration timing from an earlier section, however good, does not, and including it dilutes the argument.\n\nThe course anchors keep the memo honest about learning, not just logistics:\n\n- **UDL learner variability** is often the decisive evidence in a tool memo, because an accessibility failure is a categorical reason a stakeholder must understand, not a minor scoring difference.\n- **Cognitive load theory** and **Mayer's principles** justify the learning claims behind the recommendation, so the memo argues the tool serves learners, not merely that it is cheaper or faster to author in.\n\nA decision memo has a fixed anatomy, and each part does one job. Match each part to its function.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Header\",\"definition\":\"Names the audience and the decision requested, so the reader knows immediately what they are being asked to approve\"},{\"term\":\"Recommendation (BLUF)\",\"definition\":\"States the bottom line in one opening sentence, including the chief alternative it beats, signaling a real comparison happened\"},{\"term\":\"Evidence\",\"definition\":\"Each piece (audit score, pilot result, budget) attached to the specific claim it supports, not dumped as a list\"},{\"term\":\"Trade-off acknowledged\",\"definition\":\"Names what the recommendation costs and why that cost is acceptable, distinguishing an argument from a sales pitch\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A memo opens: 'In Module 2, I studied affordances, interaction patterns, feedback loops, and AI boundaries, built a storyboard, ran a pilot, and recommend Tool A.' Why does this fail as a decision memo even if the work was excellent?\" explanation=\"It is ordered chronologically (activity-order) and buries the recommendation in the final clause, names no audience, and ties no evidence to a claim. A memo is judged as a decision instrument: it must lead with the recommendation and link each piece of evidence to the point it supports, not recount effort.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is fine; thoroughness in listing the work is the mark of quality\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It recounts activity in discovery order and buries the recommendation, forcing the reader to reconstruct the argument\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The only problem is that it should mention more Module 2 findings\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Revising a Design Memo\n\nHere is a before-and-after of a Module 2 design memo that synthesizes the module's decisions for a stakeholder.\n\n**Before (activity-log memo)**\n\n> In Module 2, I studied platform affordances, interaction patterns, feedback loops, and AI boundaries. I built a storyboard, ran a small pilot, and made some revisions. I also compared a few authoring tools. Overall the project went well, and I recommend Tool A.\n\nThis recounts activity, hides the recommendation in the last clause, names no audience, and never ties the pilot or the audit to a claim.\n\n**The decision point.** The memo's real job is to help the program lead decide which authoring tool to fund for a cohort that includes screen-reader users, on a fixed budget.\n\n**After (argument memo)**\n\n> To: Program Lead. Decision requested: approve Tool A for the Summer onboarding build.\n>\n> Recommendation (bottom line up front): Fund Tool A, not Tool B, despite Tool B's larger template library.\n>\n> Why: Our learner cohort includes screen-reader users, so accessibility is a non-negotiable gate. In the comparative audit, Tool B scored 0 on screen-reader support while Tool A scored 2; the pilot confirmed 3 of 5 low-vision testers could not complete Tool B's branching step. Tool A also stayed within the fixed budget where Tool C did not.\n>\n> Trade-off acknowledged: Tool A has fewer templates, which adds about 4 hours of authoring time. I judge that cost acceptable against an accessibility failure.\n\nNotice the moves: a named audience and decision, a bottom-line-up-front recommendation, and each piece of evidence (the audit score, the 3-of-5 pilot result, the budget) tied to the specific claim it supports. That is what turns a Module 2 summary into a memo a stakeholder can act on.\n\nRebuild the memo from activity-log to argument one move at a time, predicting each before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"From activity log to argument memo\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The draft ends with the clause and I recommend Tool A. Applying BLUF, where does the recommendation belong and what must it include?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"At the end, after the full activity narrative\",\"In the opening line, including the chief alternative it beats (Tool A, not Tool B)\",\"Anywhere, as long as it appears once\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"BLUF puts the recommendation first and names the alternative it beats. Fund Tool A, not Tool B, despite Tool B larger template library signals that a real comparison happened, before any reasoning is read.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Module 2 produced many findings, including narration timing from an earlier section. Which evidence belongs in this memo?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"All of it, because comprehensiveness shows effort\",\"Only the evidence that bears on the Tool A versus Tool B decision: the accessibility gate, the pilot result, the budget\",\"Whatever fills the page\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Selectivity is the synthesis being tested. The accessibility gate and the 3-of-5 low-vision pilot result bear on the tool choice; narration timing, however good, dilutes the argument and is cut.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The trade-off section (Tool A has fewer templates, adding about 4 hours of authoring, acceptable against an accessibility failure) strengthens the memo rather than weakening it: naming the cost shows it was weighed, which is what makes a reader trust the recommendation.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nLead with the recommendation, name the audience and decision, and attach every piece of evidence to the point it supports. If a sentence does not advance or qualify the recommendation, it does not belong in the memo.\n\nBefore you send a design memo, test it against these questions:\n\n1. **Audience and decision:** Does the first line name who must decide and what you are asking them to approve?\n2. **BLUF:** Is the recommendation in the opening, including the main alternative it beats?\n3. **Linked evidence:** Is each piece of evidence tied to a specific claim, with no findings included that the decision does not need?\n4. **Trade-off:** Does the memo name what the recommendation costs and argue why that cost is acceptable?\n\nA memo that passes lets a busy stakeholder act in minutes. A memo that recites activity makes them do the synthesis you were supposed to do.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading equates length and thoroughness with quality, so the memo recounts everything Module 2 produced. A stakeholder does not need the complete activity log; they need the subset of evidence that bears on the decision, organized as an argument. Comprehensiveness is a virtue in your design files and a defect in your memo.\n\nThe second misreading treats acknowledging a trade-off as a weakness that undercuts the recommendation. The opposite is true: a memo that admits Tool A costs about four extra hours of authoring, and argues that cost is acceptable against an accessibility failure, is more persuasive than one that pretends the choice was free. Naming the cost shows you weighed it, which is exactly what makes a reader trust the recommendation.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nCompare the two memos in the worked example structurally. The \"before\" version is chronological: it follows the order in which the designer did the work, which is the order least useful to a reader. The \"after\" version is argumentative: it opens with the header and recommendation, then supplies reasoning, then acknowledges the trade-off. The same underlying facts, reordered from activity-order to argument-order, transform an effort log into a decision instrument.\n\nTrace the claim-to-evidence links in the revised memo. \"Tool B scored 0 on screen-reader support while Tool A scored 2\" is attached directly to the accessibility claim; \"the pilot confirmed 3 of 5 low-vision testers could not complete Tool B's branching step\" converts that score from an abstraction into a lived consequence; \"Tool A stayed within the fixed budget where Tool C did not\" eliminates the remaining candidate. Each sentence does one job, and no Module 2 finding appears unless it bears on the Tool A decision. That selectivity is the synthesis the memo is testing.\n\nThe walkthrough's lesson is that synthesis is a writing discipline as much as a thinking one. You can hold all the right findings in your head and still produce a memo a stakeholder cannot act on, if you present them in the order you discovered them rather than the order that builds the argument.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read professional writing this week (an executive summary, a recommendation email, a one-pager), find the recommendation and time how long it takes you to reach it. The good ones put it first; the weak ones make you hunt. Train yourself to feel that hunt as friction, because it is exactly the friction a stakeholder feels when reading a memo of yours that buries the bottom line.\n\nA strong reader of design memos can answer four questions about any recommendation document:\n\n1. Who is the audience, and what specific decision is requested?\n2. Is the recommendation up front, with the alternative it beats named?\n3. Is every piece of evidence linked to a claim, with irrelevant findings cut?\n4. Is the chief trade-off acknowledged and argued, or hidden?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong artifact for this section is a memo with a named audience and decision, a BLUF recommendation that names the alternative it beats, evidence linked claim by claim, and an acknowledged trade-off. The strongest versions draw their evidence from across Module 2 (the affordance check, the audit gate, the pilot) while ruthlessly excluding findings the decision does not need, demonstrating synthesis rather than recital.\n\nWhen you bring a draft to BigAL, ask it to read as the busy stakeholder. A useful prompt is: \"You are the program lead with five minutes. Read this memo and tell me, in one sentence, what I am asking you to approve and your single biggest unanswered question.\" If the AI cannot restate your ask, your BLUF has failed; the gap it names is the evidence link you forgot to make.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nKeep the same Module 2 evidence but change the audience from a program lead who controls budget to a faculty committee that cares about pedagogy and academic policy. The recommendation may hold, but the memo must be re-argued, because what counts as decisive evidence shifts with the audience. For the program lead, the budget line and the four extra authoring hours were central; for the faculty committee, the pilot's learning evidence and the accessibility gate move to the front, while the authoring-hours cost recedes. The same facts, re-selected and re-ordered for a different decider, produce a different memo. If you can explain why the budget detail leads for one audience and the learning evidence leads for another, you understand that a design memo is an argument addressed to a specific reader, not a fixed summary of what you did.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain in your own words why acknowledging a trade-off (Tool A costs about four extra authoring hours) makes a memo more persuasive rather than less.\" answer=\"A recommendation that admits no cost reads like a sales pitch and invites the reader to find the hidden downside themselves, which erodes trust. Naming the cost and arguing why it is acceptable (four extra hours against an accessibility failure) shows the writer weighed the alternative honestly and still chose Tool A. That demonstrated weighing is exactly what a stakeholder needs to see to act on the recommendation, so the acknowledged trade-off is a source of credibility, not a concession that weakens the case.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/02-08-module-2-design-memo-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"02.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 02.08. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo", "stem": "A design memo opens: \"First I built the storyboard, then I ran a pilot, then I revised the feedback.\" Why does this fail as a design memo?", "options": ["It is too short to count as a memo.", "It recounts activity rather than arguing for a recommended decision the reader must act on.", "It mentions a pilot, which does not belong in a memo.", "Memos should never be written in the first person."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A design memo makes an argument for a decision. A chronological activity log gives the reader nothing to decide.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_memo_is_summary", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo", "stem": "A memo argues well but names no recipient and no decision. What does it most need?", "options": ["More citations to course readings.", "A named audience and the specific decision that audience owns (for example, the program lead deciding whether to fund the screen-reader build).", "A longer background section.", "A different font for the heading."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A memo to no one, about no decision, cannot be acted on. Naming the audience and the decision they own makes it actionable.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_no_named_audience", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo", "stem": "A reviewer cannot find the recommendation until the last paragraph of a one-page memo. What revision principle applies?", "options": ["Move the recommendation to the front as a bottom-line-up-front statement, then let the background support it.", "Keep the recommendation last so it lands as a strong finish.", "Add a summary paragraph that repeats the entire memo.", "Remove the background entirely so only the recommendation remains."], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "Bottom line up front lets a busy reader act immediately. Background should support the recommendation, not delay it.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_recommendation_buried", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_module_2_design_memo"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A memo recommends adopting Tool A and attaches pilot data and an audit matrix as appendices, but the body never references them. What is the strongest evidence-based revision?", "options": ["Delete the appendices to shorten the memo.", "Tie each evidence item to the claim it supports in the body (for example, cite the 3-of-5 pilot miss when arguing the accessibility gate), so the data becomes the argument rather than decoration.", "Move the appendices to the front of the memo.", "Add more appendices to look thorough."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Unlinked evidence is decoration. Connecting each data point to the claim it backs turns attachments into the memo's actual argument.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_evidence_not_linked", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_memo_is_summary", "pattern": "memo_as_activity_log", "description": "Writing the design memo as a chronological summary of what was done instead of an argument for a recommended decision", "trigger": "learner narrates the steps taken across the module without stating a recommendation a reader must act on", "feedback": "A design memo argues, it does not recount. Lead with the recommendation and the decision it answers, then use the module work as supporting evidence.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_no_named_audience", "pattern": "memo_without_decision_owner", "description": "Writing the memo with no named audience or decision owner, so it is unclear who must decide what", "trigger": "learner addresses the memo to no one and names no decision the reader is being asked to make", "feedback": "Name the audience and the decision they own. A memo to no one, about no decision, cannot be acted on.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_recommendation_buried", "pattern": "recommendation_buried_in_prose", "description": "Hiding the recommendation in the last paragraph so a busy reader cannot find what is being proposed", "trigger": "learner places the actual recommendation only at the end after several paragraphs of background", "feedback": "Put the recommendation up front (a BLUF, bottom line up front). Background supports the recommendation; it should not bury it.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_2_8_module_2_design_memo_evidence_not_linked", "pattern": "evidence_listed_not_tied", "description": "Listing module evidence without tying each piece to the specific claim it supports in the recommendation", "trigger": "learner appends pilot data or audit results that are never connected to the recommended decision", "feedback": "Tie each evidence item to the claim it backs. Unlinked data is decoration; linked data is the argument.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}